#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2f4e51c-127b-53ff-806a-0c84f06270f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305011} {:answer/id #uuid "84d98ff2-f561-5fce-9f97-55116b362885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305012} {:answer/id #uuid "55b304d2-3ae3-529e-8107-dbea84d7880c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305013} {:answer/id #uuid "b20e16f3-6b33-5faa-b19e-dd59e381b293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305014} {:answer/id #uuid "e275a98c-4665-5001-9112-ee361c3c9c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-4)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b304d2-3ae3-529e-8107-dbea84d7880c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305013} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cf51bee-fb6f-5fed-94ba-81c4cae4cc19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a3cabfd-8e04-5d9b-aca6-d49b67766ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-132687" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/37c80a68fc1ee7dd534c795a7cfae13e44f63b09df4f2e948b29acbd1fb7ff06.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-4$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-4)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-1)<0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-3)<{f}^{′}(-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cb9ad38-fab0-5865-8f12-25906774fb82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cb9ad38-fab0-5865-8f12-25906774fb82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305018} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd94be4-28fc-50b5-82d9-54cc9576b64d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305017} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8dbaea-f198-5983-ba66-4c61643200f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b8dbaea-f198-5983-ba66-4c61643200f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305020} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b8fa25b-4190-542f-967c-5ecc18656dcc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a451281a-7cb5-5533-914f-03710ddd18f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-316736" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/6c3a45ac200d1f65c326354297e6e03b5347b4d4ec3fbb2a99bb7cde98376db8.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}$ (plotted above) is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3x}\\cdot 3)x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$ is the domain of $g$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for $x>0,$ the denominator is positive; thus this reduces to\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)<0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{e}{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, this critical point is a maximum. The slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{e}{3}) & \\frac{e}{3} & (\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ) \\\\ g & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {g}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec45d8d6-8aa8-53f6-bc17-33be34484578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305023} {:answer/id #uuid "50043f23-68dc-50c7-99ba-4ec2207e390a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec45d8d6-8aa8-53f6-bc17-33be34484578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305023} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a681c15-566d-5a7c-a7a5-a235d5ecd1d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305022} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d355f52-cec2-57fc-b0bd-143c40abdc5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d355f52-cec2-57fc-b0bd-143c40abdc5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305026} :answer-field/id #uuid "92db79fb-3ce2-5590-9b3e-79e6af7b599f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40fd8bf3-679e-5c97-a32d-0db3134faa3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-316787" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}+2x\\,{e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ for all real $x,$ this reduces to\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x+3)(x-1)$ has roots at $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/9b847390fb6de568144d309c45fb8c904475bdde79a46a90ec0fd4426a71b57b.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x+3)(x-1)$ is positive when $x<-3$ or $x>1.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x>1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f9a5c4-dfa7-503e-8dae-ec6e794546df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47f9a5c4-dfa7-503e-8dae-ec6e794546df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305029} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7604896-01f4-554f-99d0-23623f063e9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305028} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c45121de-0b9c-5161-82db-2bf44134d2d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45121de-0b9c-5161-82db-2bf44134d2d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305031} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7c4e82-cf1c-5459-b526-90f09a4e0d43" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74254c39-aaac-5f10-9749-e2334224a425" :question/math-academy-id "q-343574" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+2$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =(x-2)\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)\\sqrt{x}<0.\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\sqrt{x}$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-2<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98089e50-003a-56d5-8b94-527484619f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104250} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ba40f6-51c5-5e69-af57-ce55912dc1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(-1) < f^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104251} {:answer/id #uuid "f2812a87-c11a-5b76-abda-59b8ee615fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(-1) < f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104252} {:answer/id #uuid "30078066-257d-541d-a43c-7dd457cf8b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(-1) < f^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104253} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad3b6c0-018e-5c91-b03b-d60c465306bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(-1) < f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1ba40f6-51c5-5e69-af57-ce55912dc1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(-1) < f^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104251} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69e51c6-498c-52c4-a465-b3753253cb81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d7369bb-276c-5f8c-818f-dc228484ba99" :question/math-academy-id "q-49005" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/5e232394bb3f520386068110e42887faa288c31077a660570494ccc5a85b0486.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(0)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(1)>0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1).$"}]}
